1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,931
You have an agent skill that works. Today
the only way to sell it is to hand someone

2
00:00:04,931 --> 00:00:09,745
your prompt and your API key, or to bill
them thirty cents to process a call worth

3
00:00:09,745 --> 00:00:12,446
four hundredths of a
cent. So nobody sells it.

4
00:00:13,646 --> 00:00:17,389
ARCADE turns a skill into a
paid endpoint. One command.

5
00:00:17,389 --> 00:00:22,765
You get paid per call in USDC on Arc,
and your code, your prompts and your keys

6
00:00:22,765 --> 00:00:24,466
never leave your machine.

7
00:00:25,666 --> 00:00:31,030
The buyer here is an agent. It finds what
is for sale and asks what one call costs.

8
00:00:31,030 --> 00:00:34,778
Listing, describing and quoting
are free and sign nothing.

9
00:00:34,778 --> 00:00:40,206
And the price it reads comes from
the endpoint's own payment challenge, not from the

10
00:00:40,206 --> 00:00:40,852
catalogue.

11
00:00:41,852 --> 00:00:46,042
The endpoint answers with HTTP
402 and the exact terms it wants.

12
00:00:46,042 --> 00:00:50,690
Now the buyer's wallet signs an
EIP-3009 authorization, in the browser.

13
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:54,226
Five milliseconds, no gas,
no round trip to the chain.

14
00:00:54,226 --> 00:00:57,630
ARCADE never holds the funds
and never sees the key.

15
00:00:57,630 --> 00:01:02,933
The address being paid is shown in full,
because verifying who gets your money is

16
00:01:02,933 --> 00:01:04,701
the whole job of this card.

17
00:01:05,701 --> 00:01:10,047
The work runs on the seller's own machine.
The output is validated against the

18
00:01:10,047 --> 00:01:13,781
listing's own schema, and only then
is the authorization broadcast.

19
00:01:14,181 --> 00:01:20,139
USDC moves on Arc. Here is the transaction:
one cent in, and nought point nine five

20
00:01:20,139 --> 00:01:25,810
of a cent out to the seller. If the job
had refused, timed out, or returned the

21
00:01:25,810 --> 00:01:29,184
wrong shape, nothing
would have been broadcast.

22
00:01:29,184 --> 00:01:34,568
That is the refund — there is nothing
to refund, because nothing was taken.

23
00:01:35,968 --> 00:01:40,929
And a skill can buy, too. Mid-job, this
one needed a fact it did not have, so it

24
00:01:40,929 --> 00:01:45,951
bought it from another listing on the same
marketplace — inside the job the buyer

25
00:01:45,951 --> 00:01:51,036
had already paid for. One buyer action,
two settlements, each with its own receipt

26
00:01:51,036 --> 00:01:56,120
and its own fee split. And the sub-purchase
is bounded by a process the skill does

27
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:58,352
not control, so it cannot overspend.

28
00:01:59,752 --> 00:02:03,724
The hub cannot see the skill.
Not because a filter runs.

29
00:02:03,724 --> 00:02:09,682
The published type has nowhere to put a prompt
or a key, so leaking one is a compile

30
00:02:09,682 --> 00:02:14,150
error, and a property test asserts
it over generated manifests.

31
00:02:14,150 --> 00:02:19,824
And the runner dials out, so a seller can
sell from a laptop behind NAT, with no

32
00:02:19,824 --> 00:02:20,604
open ports.

33
00:02:21,604 --> 00:02:25,074
Built on Circle's stack,
on Circle's own chain.

34
00:02:25,074 --> 00:02:30,390
On Arc, USDC is both the native gas
token and the ERC-20 our prices use.

35
00:02:30,390 --> 00:02:35,042
Gateway nanopayments ship as a
second, conformance-tested rail.

36
00:02:35,042 --> 00:02:37,626
And Circle's own CLI reads our 402.

37
00:02:38,826 --> 00:02:42,549
Publish a skill, get paid
per call, and keep your code.

38
00:02:42,549 --> 00:02:47,626
ARCADE is live on Arc testnet now. The
marketplace, the receipts, and every

39
00:02:47,626 --> 00:02:48,439
transaction.
